/*!
    \page qtros2-getting-started.html
    \title Getting Started with Qt ROS2
    \brief Installing ROS 2 and Qt, building the module, and writing your
    first publisher.

    Qt ROS2 Bridge lets a Qt application join a ROS 2 graph and exchange
    messages directly from QML. This page takes you from a clean machine to a
    running application that publishes a topic.

    \note This documentation describes a build against \b{ROS 2 \rosdistro}.
    The commands below name that distribution explicitly. If you install a
    different one, substitute its name. See the \rosdocslink.

    \section1 Prerequisites

    \list
        \li Ubuntu 24.04 or 26.04.
        \li ROS 2 \rosdistro.
        \li Qt 6.8 or later, including the Qt Quick and Qt Qml modules.
        \li A C++ toolchain, CMake, Git, and Ninja.
    \endlist

    Install the toolchain packages first:

    \badcode
    sudo apt update
    sudo apt install -y build-essential cmake git ninja-build
    \endcode

    \section1 Installing ROS 2

    Add the ROS 2 apt repository by following the installation instructions in
    the \rosdocslink, then install the desktop and development packages:

    \badcode
    sudo apt install -y ros-jazzy-desktop ros-dev-tools
    \endcode

    Source the ROS 2 environment in every shell that builds or runs Qt ROS2:

    \badcode
    source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash
    \endcode

    \section1 Installing Qt

    Install Qt 6.8 or later with the online installer from
    \l{https://www.qt.io/download-qt-installer}{qt.io}. Select the
    \e{Desktop gcc 64-bit} component together with the Qt Quick and Qt Qml
    modules.

    \section1 Building the module

    Qt ROS2 builds as a Qt module with \c qt-configure-module. CMake has to
    find the ROS 2 libraries, and there are two ways to arrange that.

    The first way sources the ROS 2 environment, which is the option to prefer
    for day-to-day development:

    \badcode
    source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash

    mkdir -p ~/ros2bridge_build && cd ~/ros2bridge_build
    ~/Qt/6.12.0/gcc_64/bin/qt-configure-module /path/to/qt-ros2-bridge

    cmake --build . --parallel
    cmake --install .
    \endcode

    The second way passes the path explicitly, which suits continuous
    integration and any environment where sourcing a shell script is awkward:

    \badcode
    mkdir -p ~/ros2bridge_build && cd ~/ros2bridge_build
    ~/Qt/6.12.0/gcc_64/bin/qt-configure-module /path/to/qt-ros2-bridge \
        -DROS2_PATH=/opt/ros/jazzy

    cmake --build . --parallel
    cmake --install .
    \endcode

    Adjust the Qt path to match your installation.

    \note \c{cmake --install .} applies to prefix builds, which means any build
    against an installed Qt. An in-tree build, where this module builds as part
    of a Qt source tree, needs no install step because the files land in the
    build tree already.

    \section1 Setting up an application project

    An application links the \c Ros2Core module and declares which ROS 2
    capabilities and message modules it needs:

    \code
    find_package(Qt6 REQUIRED COMPONENTS Quick Ros2Core)

    qt_standard_project_setup(REQUIRES 6.8)

    qt_add_executable(apphello main.cpp)

    qt_add_qml_module(apphello
        URI hello
        QML_FILES
            Main.qml
    )

    qt_ros2_configure_target(apphello
        CAPABILITIES PUBLISHER
        MODULES QtRos2GeometryMessages
    )

    target_link_libraries(apphello PRIVATE Qt6::Quick)
    \endcode

    \c qt_ros2_configure_target takes three lists:

    \list
        \li \c CAPABILITIES declares what the application does. Valid values
            are \c MESSAGES, \c PUBLISHER, \c SUBSCRIBER, \c SERVICE,
            \c SERVER, and \c ACTION.
        \li \c MODULES names the generated message modules to link, such as
            \c QtRos2GeometryMessages or \c QtRos2StandardServices.
        \li \c IMPORT_PACKAGES names third-party ROS 2 interface packages to
            wrap, for packages that Qt ROS2 does not ship a module for.
    \endlist

    \section1 Writing your first publisher

    Everything that faces ROS 2 lives inside a \c Node. A publisher is a child
    of the node, and the node owns the underlying \c rclcpp publisher and tears
    it down for you:

    \snippet qml/publisher.qml 0

    Three details matter here:

    \list
        \li Message value types are constructible from plain JavaScript
            objects, so you never build a message imperatively. Field names use
            Qt's \c camelCase spelling, so \c frameId rather than \c frame_id.
        \li \c{rosNode.initialized} guards the button, so the application
            cannot publish before the ROS 2 context is ready.
        \li \c PoseStampedPublisher is a stamped publisher, so it fills
            \c header.stamp from the ROS 2 node clock at publish time. That is
            why the snippet leaves the stamp unset.
    \endlist

    A QML application does not declare a context. Declaring a \c Node
    initializes the process-wide context on demand. A C++ application that
    creates entities before loading any QML, or that forwards ROS 2
    command-line arguments, calls \l{QRos2Context::init()} from \c main()
    instead.

    \section1 Running the application

    \list 1
        \li Source the ROS 2 environment in the shell you run from.
        \li Build and run the application.
        \li Watch the topic from a second, ROS-sourced terminal:
            \badcode
            ros2 topic echo /hello_pose
            \endcode
    \endlist

    Each press of the button prints one message in the second terminal.

    \section1 Developing with Qt Creator

    Launch Qt Creator from a shell that has already sourced the ROS 2
    environment, so it inherits the paths to the ROS 2 libraries and the
    generated QML modules:

    \badcode
    source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash
    ~/Qt/Tools/QtCreator/bin/qtcreator
    \endcode

    Rebuild the module after you change \c Ros2Core, change a generator
    template, or wrap a new message package. Application development against
    the generated types needs no rebuild of the module.

    \section1 Next steps

    \l{Simple Publisher} is the next step up from the snippet above: the same
    publisher, wrapped in a UI that lets you edit the topic and frame, watch
    the subscriber count, and see each message as you send it.

    \image simple-publisher-startup.webp {The Simple Publisher example at
           startup, showing an editable topic and frame, a Publish Random Pose
           button, an Automatic time stamp switch, and a prompt to press the
           button to publish a PoseStamped message}

    \list
        \li \l{Simple Publisher} and \l{Simple Subscriber} show both ends of a
            topic in two windows.
        \li \l{Choosing a Communication Pattern} explains when to reach for a
            topic, a service, an action, or a parameter.
        \li \l{Qt ROS2 Examples} lists every example.
        \li \l{Qt ROS2 QML Types} is the API reference.
    \endlist

    \sa {Qt ROS2 Examples}, {Choosing a Communication Pattern}
*/
